{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-2988df17-6f8d-4ae5-b176-f0e57c4f94c5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd425718cf4411a0","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8008-fmmxljnz/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8008-E003.md","content":"# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA in odh-model-controller\n\n## Description\n\nProvides the downstream authentication context that KEDA ScaledObjects need to query\nThanos/Prometheus on OpenShift for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA deployments with\nPrometheus-type triggers. On OpenShift, KEDA cannot query Prometheus without an\nauthenticated context; this epic adds a parallel reconciler flow in odh-model-controller\nthat provisions the per-namespace authentication resources, following the existing\n`kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern used for v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n\n## Scope\n\n- A reconciler flow in odh-model-controller (extending the pattern at\n  `internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go`) that reacts to\n  LLMInferenceService resources with the standalone KEDA path configured and\n  Prometheus-type triggers, and provisions per-namespace authentication resources:\n  - a dedicated ServiceAccount (`inference-prometheus-auth`),\n  - a ServiceAccountToken Secret (`kubernetes.io/service-account-token` type),\n  - a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access,\n  - a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying the bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's\n    Prometheus triggers.\n- Resource naming, owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup consistent with the\n  existing v1beta1 reconciler (resources are cleaned up when the owning\n  LLMInferenceService is deleted or its KEDA configuration removed).\n- Any RBAC or TLS adjustments for OpenShift identified by the upstream adaptation\n  investigation, folded into this reconciler flow as additional scope.\n- TLS 1.2+ for Prometheus/Thanos queries via the TriggerAuthentication CR (no new\n  authentication surfaces introduced).\n\nOut of scope: the llmisvc-controller ScaledObject creation itself, KEDA installation\n(platform policy overlay 0008 — documented as a prerequisite), and documentation of the\nTriggerAuthentication reference in user trigger definitions (documentation work stream).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an LLMInferenceService with `spec.scaling.keda` including a Prometheus-type\n  trigger, when the odh-model-controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication\n  resources are created (ServiceAccount, token Secret, Role/RoleBinding,\n  TriggerAuthentication CR), measured by `kubectl get` on each resource type in the\n  target namespace.\n- Given a created TriggerAuthentication CR, when KEDA evaluates the Prometheus trigger,\n  then the query succeeds with the ServiceAccount bearer token over TLS 1.2+, measured by\n  KEDA ScaledObject reporting the trigger as active and returning metric values.\n- Given an LLMInferenceService is deleted or its standalone KEDA configuration removed,\n  when the controller reconciles, then the owned authentication resources are cleaned up,\n  measured by no orphaned ServiceAccounts, Secrets, RoleBindings, or\n  TriggerAuthentication CRs remaining.\n- Deployments without Prometheus-type triggers, without standalone KEDA configured, or\n  with WVA configured are unaffected, measured by no authentication resources created for\n  them.\n- Any RBAC/TLS adjustments for OpenShift surfaced by the upstream adaptation\n  investigation are implemented (or explicitly recorded as not required in the findings).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without WVA — this\n  epic enables the primary supported trigger source (Prometheus) for the standalone path\n  on OpenShift.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_seque