{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-280e4974-3c76-4161-906d-410b039d61f6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9abb80fa7f44213","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8008-fmmxljnz/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8008-E004.md","content":"# LLMInferenceService autoscaling documentation: decision guide, recipes, and troubleshooting\n\n## Description\n\nAuthors the operator-facing documentation for LLMInferenceService autoscaling covering\nboth paths — WVA-mediated autoscaling and standalone KEDA — plus static allocation,\ndelivering the decision guide, per-workload-shape configuration recipes, troubleshooting\nguidance, and the supported configuration matrix promised by the strategy.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Autoscaling decision guide: WVA-mediated autoscaling vs. standalone KEDA vs. static\n  allocation, with use-case-based recommendations (e.g., the coding-assistant scenario\n  that scales prefill/decode pools during business hours and releases GPUs overnight) and\n  clear guidance on when each path fits, addressing the \"two scaling paths in a single\n  CRD may confuse operators\" risk.\n- Configuration recipes per workload shape, including the standalone KEDA spec fields\n  (`pollingInterval`, `cooldownPeriod`, `triggers`) and examples for Resource, External,\n  and PodMetric trigger types.\n- Scale-to-zero and scale-from-zero guidance: how `idleReplicaCount: 0` releases GPU\n  capacity, and recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations using EPP flow control\n  queue metrics, including example ScaledObject templates (mitigating the\n  misconfigured-trigger reliability risk).\n- Realistic cold-start expectations for scale-from-zero latency, referencing the fast\n  model startup RFEs (RHAIRFE-7753, RHAIRFE-7599, RHAIRFE-7139) and headroom\n  configuration recommendations for latency-sensitive workloads, aligned to the agreed\n  p50/p95 scale-from-zero targets.\n- KEDA as a documented prerequisite: RHOAI does not auto-install KEDA (overlay 0008);\n  installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository (ODH-ADR-0007).\n- Instructions for operators to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource in KEDA\n  trigger definitions for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n- Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments (e.g., polling-interval\n  impact at 2000-GPU fleet scale), incorporating the Performance/PSAP team's answer.\n- Troubleshooting paths and a supported configuration matrix (paths x topologies x\n  trigger types).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The decision guide covers all three allocation modes (WVA-mediated, standalone KEDA,\n  static) with use-case-based recommendations, reviewed for technical accuracy against\n  the implemented API surface.\n- Configuration recipes exist for each supported workload shape and trigger type, and\n  every example is technically reviewed against the implementation (validated against the\n  architecture context files: workload-variant-autoscaler.md, kserve.md, llm-d.md,\n  odh-model-controller.md, and overlay 0011).\n- Scale-from-zero documentation includes recommended trigger configurations using EPP\n  queue metrics with example templates, and documents realistic cold-start time\n  expectations aligned to the agreed p50/p95 scale-from-zero targets.\n- KEDA installation is documented as a prerequisite with guidance referencing the GitOps\n  repository, consistent with platform policy (no auto-installation).\n- Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments is included, reflecting the\n  Performance/PSAP input.\n- Troubleshooting guidance covers common failure modes: misconfigured triggers failing\n  the zero-to-one transition, metric outages, and CEL validation errors for invalid\n  path combinations.\n- All content passes the Red Hat docs content process including technical review by the\n  engineering teams that implemented the autoscaling paths.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-7 (P1): well-lit path documentation covering both autoscaling paths with decision\n  guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, and troubleshooting.\n- Supports HLR-4 (P0): documented recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations\n  (the mechanism itself is implemented in the controller work stream).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":40266,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5a4845098be9430","agent_id":"a4eeb449d7b52be09","session_id":"c67b7b09-8e50-4c38-b7b7-230c45313c19","uuid":"aa30112f-9674-4e95-b9b1-d66692dd1fd0","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:25:24.548Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8008"}
