wired as an acceptance criterion (AC gate) on E003, not a DAG edge. |\n56\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | Documentation describes what was built; must reflect the delivered API surface and both autoscaling paths. |\n57\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | Docs must instruct operators to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift. |\n58\t| E004 not gated by E001 | Rule 11 scope | Rule 11 blocks docs on Implementation epics only; E001 is an Investigation and is already a transitive prerequisite via E002/E003. |\n59\t\n60\tUpstream external dependency handling (Rule 21): kserve/kserve#5839 is an in-progress\n61\tthird-party upstream PR. RHOAI is not the submitting party, and a viable fallback exists\n62\t(carry the patch downstream with backport once merged, per the strategy's risk mitigation).\n63\tIt is therefore modeled as a precondition captured as an acceptance criterion on E002, plus\n64\tthe E001 investigation to resolve adaptation needs — not as a separate upstream Implementation\n65\tepic. Tiered delivery applies (see below).\n66\t\n67\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n68\t\n69\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n70\t|-----|----------|---------|\n71\t| HLR-1: Standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without WVA | P0 | E002 (API/controller path), E003 (OpenShift Prometheus trigger authentication) |\n72\t| HLR-2: Mutual exclusivity of WVA and standalone KEDA via CEL validation | P0 | E002 |\n73\t| HLR-3: Scale-to-zero via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` (both paths) | P0 | E002 |\n74\t| HLR-4: Scale-from-zero (standalone KEDA via user-configured triggers) | P0 | E002 (mechanism), E004 (recommended trigger configurations) |\n75\t| HLR-5: Disaggregated prefill/decode independent scaling | P0 | E002 |\n76\t| HLR-6: Scaling events visible with signal attribution | P1 | E002 (status conditions/events for standalone path) |\n77\t| HLR-7: Decision-guide documentation covering both paths | P1 | E004 |\n78\t\n79\tAll 7 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. Priority-split check for E002: HLR-6 (P1) is\n80\tbundled with the P0 work because scaling status conditions and events share the same\n81\tScalingSpec API surface and controller reconciliation path as the P0 work — it is not\n82\tindependently deferrable and does not warrant a separate epic.\n83\t\n84\t## Component Mapping\n85\t\n86\tCanonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n87\t- \"KServe (llmisvc-controller)\" → **Serving Orchestration** (no \"KServe\" component exists; Serving Orchestration is the closest parent-level component for the serving controllers).\n88\t- \"odh-model-controller\" → **Serving Orchestration** (same mapping rationale; RHOAI serving-stack controller).\n89\t- \"Documentation\" → **Documentation** (exact match).\n90\t\n91\tBoth KServe (kserve.md) and odh-model-controller (odh-model-controller.md) exist in\n92\t`.context/architecture-context/` — both are in-platform components, so no onboarding chain\n93\t(Rule 20) is generated. E002 and E003 remain separate epics despite sharing component and\n94\tteam because they live in different repositories with different logical changes (Rule 1/14).\n95\t\n96\t## Unknown Handling Log\n97\t\n98\t| Unknown (source) | Disposition | Rationale |\n99\t|------------------|-------------|-----------|\n100\t| Upstream PR #5839 downstream adaptation needs (Open Questions) | Investigation E001, gating E002 (`rewrite`) and E003 (`add_remediation`) | Answer changes what the downstream implementations must include. |\n101\t| Target p50/p95 for scale-from-zero time (Open Questions, llm-d team) | Acceptance criterion on E004 | Determines documented headroom recommendations only; epic structure unchanged. |\n102\t| Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet scale (Open Questions, PSAP) | Acceptance criterion on E004 | Documentation content input; no structural impact. |\n103\t| KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` pause/resume behavior for LLM workloads (Assumption, needs validation) | Acceptance criterion (validation) on E002 | Scale-to-zero is a P0 HLR regardless of outcome; failure = fix-and-retry, epic structure unchanged. |\n104\t| EPP flow control metrics availability at zero replicas (Assumption, needs validation) | Acceptance criterion (validation) on E002 and content input to E004 | Scale-from-zero trigger mechanism is user-configured either way; affects documented guidance, not epic existence. |\n105\t| Upstream PR delay (Risk, mitigation: downstream patch carry) | Acceptance criterion on E002 | Viable fallback exists (Rule 21) — noted as AC, not a separate epic. |\n106\t\n107\t## Health Warnings\n108\t\n109\t- **Scalability NFR has no measurable target**: the strategy explicitly flags that no RHOAI-specific target exists for concurrent ScaledObjects. No numeric scalability AC can be derived; E004 carries capacity guidance instead. Human verification recommended.\n110\t- **AC 6 feasibility (metric outage → hold replica count)**: holding the current replica count during a Prometheus outage is KEDA operator behavior, not something the llmisvc-controller controls. Kept as an AC on E002 but flagged: if KEDA does not natively hold replicas on metric errors, this AC may be unsatisfiable within this strategy's scope.\n111\t- **Pending process gates**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, License Validation, etc.) are marked \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. These are strategy sign-off gates, not epic-level work; they inform E001's human-judgment signal but do not create epics.\n112\t- **Priority inversion check**: none found. P0 HLRs align with the core API/controller work; the P1 observability HLR is bundled for surface-coupling reasons (documented above), not deprioritized.\n113\t\n114\t## Tiered Delivery\n115\t\n116\tExternal dependency with uncertain timing: upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 (in progress).\n117\tPartial value can be delivered without it, so the work splits:\n118\t\n119\t- **Tier 1 (independent of upstream merge timing)**:\n120\t  - E001 — resolves adaptation needs and the patch-carry decision (risk-reduction).\n121\t  - E003 — the odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication reconciler follows the existing\n122\t    `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern and can be built and unit-tested regardless of when\n123\t    #5839 lands (risk-reduction work with independent value for the auth flow).\n124\t- **Tier 2 (gated on the upstream outcome)**:\n125\t  - E002 — the llmisvc-controller standalone KEDA path; consumes upstream #5839 (merged) or\n126\t    the downstream patch carry selected by E001.\n127\t  - E004 — documentation of what was built (blocked by all implementations per Rule 11).\n128\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5a5a0b7b204004c","agent_id":"a789eec0caf1fe383","session_id":"c67b7b09-8e50-4c38-b7b7-230c45313c19","uuid":"47a3fff4-363f-4c3b-9fd4-f4ca65f58374","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:28:24.198Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f6d2c372e30449c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
