bound (mitigated by upstream PR #5839 as reference).'\n32\terror: null\n33\t---\n34\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-8008\n35\t\n36\t## Review Summary\n37\t\n38\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n39\tRecommendation: revise\n40\t\n41\tThe decomposition is strong on coverage, typing, gating metadata, and traceability. One\n42\tstructural defect in the DAG forces a fail: the E002 ∥ E003 parallel claim was justified\n43\tonly by examining the TriggerAuthentication direction and missed that E003's code builds\n44\tagainst the kserve v1alpha2 API types that E002's scope introduces — a schema/API\n45\tconsumption discoverable from epic content and confirmed by architecture context.\n46\t\n47\t## Criterion Details\n48\t\n49\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n50\t\n51\tAll 7 HLRs map to epics (HLR-1..5 P0 → E002/E003; HLR-6 P1 → E002; HLR-7 P1 → E004).\n52\tTraceability matrix present in the summary and per-epic HLR sections exist in every epic\n53\tfile. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (Investigation blocking all P0 work) is P0;\n54\tE002/E003 are P0; E004 is `docs-authoring` and exempt (strategy priority Critical → P0,\n55\tcorrectly derived). Priority collapse check on E002 (P1 HLR-6 bundled with P0 HLRs 1-5)\n56\tis justified: scaling status conditions share the ScalingSpec API surface and the same\n57\tcontroller reconciliation path as the P0 work — incidental polish on the P0 change, not a\n58\tdistinctly deferrable feature.\n59\t\n60\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n61\t\n62\tDiagram and frontmatter are mutually consistent (E001→E002, E001→E003, E002→E004,\n63\tE003→E004; arrows drawn dependency→dependent; every edge has a matching frontmatter\n64\t`dependencies` entry and vice versa). No cycles. Critical path 3 matches the M-standard\n65\theuristic (3-4). Rule 11 edges to the docs epic are valid and correctly do not trigger\n66\tpriority inheritance. Rule 21 handling of the upstream PR (precondition AC on E002 + E001\n67\tinvestigation + tiered delivery, no separate upstream epic) is correct.\n68\t\n69\tHowever, there is a missing data/artifact dependency between E002 and E003:\n70\t\n71\t- E003's scope states its reconciler \"reacts to LLMInferenceService resources with the\n72\t  standalone KEDA path configured and Prometheus-type triggers\" — i.e., it reads\n73\t  `spec.scaling.keda`, the API surface E002's scope explicitly introduces (\"API type\n74\t  changes in `pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_types.go`\").\n75\t- Architecture context confirms odh-model-controller declares `github.com/kserve/kserve`\n76\t  as a Go module dependency, already runs an LLMInferenceService controller, and already\n77\t  serves a v1alpha2 llminferenceservice mutating webhook — so typed access to the new\n78\t  field requires the updated kserve API types.\n79\t- The DAG justification for E002 ∥ E003 (\"different repos, no build-against artifact\")\n80\t  only analyzed the TriggerAuthentication direction (E002 not building against E003's\n81\t  output). It never examined the reverse direction: E003 building against the API/CRD\n82\t  types E002 delivers. Rule 13 explicitly lists APIs and CRDs as build-against artifacts\n83\t  requiring serial edges — this is not a configuration reference like an image digest or\n84\t  endpoint URL.\n85\t- Neither a `dependencies` edge nor a precondition AC on E003 captures the kserve\n86\t  API-type availability (E002 got a precondition AC for the upstream PR; E003 got\n87\t  nothing equivalent). In the downstream-carry outcome that E001 may select, the carried\n88\t  API types are E002's deliverable and E003 cannot compile typed code against\n89\t  `spec.scaling.keda` without them.\n90\t- The Tiered Delivery section's claim that E003 \"can be built and unit-tested regardless\n91\t  of when #5839 lands\" is contradicted by this module dependency (the auth-resource\n92\t  provisioning logic is independent, but the trigger-detection path is not).\n93\t\n94\tThis is a critical issue: a missing edge where a data/artifact dependency exists,\n95\tdiscoverable from epic content. At minimum the revision must either wire the dependency\n96\t(conditional on the E001 outcome) or add an explicit precondition AC on E003 for the\n97\tkserve API types, and correct the \"no build-against artifact\" justification and the\n98\tTier-1 independence claim.\n99\t\n100\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n101\t\n102\tE002 (kserve llmisvc-controller) and E003 (odh-model-controller) share component and\n103\tteam but are correctly split: different repositories, different logical changes (Rule 2\n104\tarchitectural sub-system boundary; Rule 14 different repos). E004 is correctly separated\n105\talong the work-product type boundary (content authoring vs. code) and team. No scope\n106\tduplication: E002 explicitly excludes TriggerAuthentication provisioning; E003\n107\texplicitly excludes ScaledObject creation. No work-product type mixing within any epic.\n108\tMinor note: E002 bundles API types + CEL validation + controller branching + status\n109\tconditions + scale-to-zero/disaggregated support + cross-topology integration testing —\n110\tthe strategy estimates this chunk at 1-2 sprints, so at the upper bound it brushes\n111\tagainst the ~2-week epic guideline; mitigated by upstream PR #5839 serving as reference\n112\timplementation.\n113\t\n114\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n115\t\n116\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (adaptation needs; carry-vs-wait) changes\n117\tdownstream scope, and both dependents carry `gated_by=E001` with `gate_failure_impact`\n118\tpopulated (`rewrite` for E002, `add_remediation` for E003 — both valid Rule 3 actions\n119\twith concrete fallback approaches). E002/E003/E004 are Implementations producing concrete\n120\tartifacts. Every `gated_by` target appears in the epic's direct `dependencies`. No epic\n121\tlists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`. E004 correctly has no gate.\n122\t\n123\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n124\t\n125\tType-appropriate signal sets used throughout (Investigation → 5-signal rubric on E001;\n126\tImplementation → 9-signal rubric on E002/E003/E004). Signal values in frontmatter match\n127\tthe rationale tables in all four ai-signals files. Most values are well justified\n128\t(e.g., E002 `open_questions: -1` consistent with the gating investigation; E001\n129\t`cluster_hardware_dependence: 0` correctly applying the \"controller-generated source is\n130\treadable source\" rule; E004 `human_process_gates: -1` for the mandatory docs technical\n131\treview). Two arguable calls: (a) E003 `external_dependency: 0` — the value is defensible\n132\tunder the rubric (no upstream contribution or vendor coordination is required), but the\n133\trationale's claim that the kserve API types are \"not a build dependency of this code\" is\n134\tcontradicted by the module dependency found under Criterion 2; (b) E001\n135\t`source_accessibility: 1` is borderline — the adaptation-analysis half of the question is\n136\tdiff-determined, but the merge-trajectory/carry-timing half is a PR still under review\n137\t(the rubric's 0 example), reasonably covered by `human_judgment_required: -1`.\n138\t\n139\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n140\t\n141\tEvery epic has testable given/when/then ACs with concrete measurements. Rule-mandated\n142\tACs: Rule 24 (docs technical review) present on E004; Rule 23 (rollback/feature-flag) N/A\n143\t— the change is additive, and backwards-compatibility ACs are present on E002 anyway\n144\t(static deployments and existing WVA deployments unchanged); Rule 25 N/A — no\n145\tkonflux-onboarding epics (both components exist in architecture context, verified). The\n146\tstrategy's AC 6 feasibility concern is honestly flagged inline. No epic body references\n147\tsibling epics by draft ID (references use descriptive names like \"the odh-model-controller\n148\twork stream\").\n149\t\n150\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n151\t\n152\tAll strategy acceptance criteria (1-7