{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b76af866097ee50f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n3\ttitle: Investigate upstream kserve/kserve#5839 landing and RHOAI downstream adaptation\n4\t  needs for standalone KEDA\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n6\tcomponent: Serving Orchestration\n7\tteam: Serving team\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 1\n14\t  local_runnability: 0\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n16\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Medium\n20\tai_implementability_score: 1\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Investigate upstream kserve/kserve#5839 landing and RHOAI downstream adaptation needs for standalone KEDA\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation resolves the strategy's gating open question: whether the upstream PR\n31\tkserve/kserve#5839 (which implements standalone KEDA support for LLMInferenceService per\n32\tthe accepted design kserve/kserve#5646) requires RHOAI downstream adaptation beyond the\n33\todh-model-controller authentication context, and whether/when RHOAI must carry the patch\n34\tdownstream if the upstream merge is delayed. The outcome determines the scope and approach\n35\tof both downstream implementation work streams (the llmisvc-controller standalone KEDA path\n36\tand the odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication reconciler).\n37\t\n38\t## Scope\n39\t\n40\t- Track and analyze the open upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 (diff against the v1alpha2\n41\t  API types, validation, and controller surfaces that RHOAI consumes).\n42\t- Determine what downstream adaptation is required beyond the TriggerAuthentication\n43\t  authentication context, specifically enumerating any RBAC and TLS adjustments needed\n44\t  for OpenShift.\n45\t- Assess whether the PR is expected to land as-is or whether RHOAI should carry the patch\n46\t  downstream (with backport once merged), and recommend a carry/wait decision with timing.\n47\t- Deliver written findings: an enumeration of required downstream adaptations (or an\n48\t  explicit \"auth context only\" conclusion), plus a patch-carry recommendation.\n49\t\n50\tOut of scope: implementing any of the adaptations (that belongs to the downstream\n51\timplementation work), and re-litigating the accepted upstream design.\n52\t\n53\t## Acceptance Criteria\n54\t\n55\t- Given the current state of kserve/kserve#5839, when the diff is analyzed against the\n56\t  RHOAI-consumed v1alpha2 surfaces, then a written findings document enumerates every\n57\t  downstream adaptation required beyond the authentication context, or explicitly concludes\n58\t  none are needed.\n59\t- Given the OpenShift deployment context, when RBAC and TLS surfaces are reviewed, then any\n60\t  required adjustments (roles, bindings, CA/TLS configuration for KEDA Prometheus triggers)\n61\t  are identified and described precisely enough to scope the downstream work.\n62\t- Given the upstream merge state and review trajectory, when the carry/wait assessment is\n63\t  performed, then a recommendation with rationale is produced covering: wait for upstream\n64\t  merge vs. carry the patch downstream, and the trigger conditions that would flip the\n65\t  recommendation.\n66\t- The findings are reviewed with the strategy's pending architecture review in mind and any\n67\t  conflicts are escalated as open items rather than silently resolved.\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability\n70\t\n71\t- De-risks HLR-1 (P0: standalone KEDA with user-defined triggers without WVA) — this\n72\t  investigation does not directly implement the requirement; it resolves the unknowns that\n73\t  gate both downstream implementation work streams covering HLR-1.\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"c