{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a24187e822425a0f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8008 — Inference-Aware Autoscaling for llm-d: KEDA primitives\n11\t\n12\tParent strategy: RHAISTRAT-8008 (priority: Critical, status: Refinement).\n13\t\n14\tTriage result: full decomposition. The strategy is M-effort (2-3 sprints), spans multiple\n15\tcomponents (KServe llmisvc-controller, odh-model-controller, Documentation), multiple teams\n16\t(Serving team, Documentation team), and includes a substantive documentation work stream\n17\t(decision guide, configuration recipes, troubleshooting — new content authoring, not\n18\tincidental touch-ups). Neither Step 0 escape-hatch check applies.\n19\t\n20\tStaff Engineer Input: empty (template placeholder only) — no overrides to log.\n21\t\n22\t## Epic List\n23\t\n24\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n25\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8008-E001 | Investigate upstream kserve/kserve#5839 landing and RHOAI downstream adaptation needs for standalone KEDA | Investigation | Serving team | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8008-E002 | Standalone KEDA autoscaling path for LLMInferenceService in the KServe llmisvc-controller | Implementation | Serving team | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8008-E003 | KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA in odh-model-controller | Implementation | Serving team | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8008-E004 | LLMInferenceService autoscaling documentation: decision guide, recipes, and troubleshooting | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation team | P0 |\n30\t\n31\tPriorities:\n32\t- E001 is P0 because it gates all P0 implementation work (an epic blocking all P0 work is implicitly P0).\n33\t- E002 covers HLRs 1-6 (five P0, one P1 bundled — see Health Warnings).\n34\t- E003 enables the primary supported trigger source (Prometheus) for the P0 standalone KEDA path (AC 1 measures a Prometheus trigger).\n35\t- E004 is a `docs-authoring` epic: exempt from priority inheritance; strategy frontmatter priority is Critical → P0.\n36\t\n37\t## Dependency DAG\n38\t\n39\t```mermaid\n40\tgraph TD\n41\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-8008-E001 Investigation: upstream PR + downstream adaptation] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-8008-E002 llmisvc-controller standalone KEDA]\n42\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-8008-E003 odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication]\n43\t    E002 --> E003\n44\t    E002 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-8008-E004 autoscaling documentation]\n45\t    E003 --> E004\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\tCritical path length: 4 (E001 → E002 → E003 → E004).\n49\t\n50\t## DAG Justification\n51\t\n52\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n53\t|------|------|-----------|\n54\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates affected Implementations) | Open question \"Does upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 require downstream adaptation beyond authentication context?\" changes what the llmisvc-controller work must include (RBAC/TLS adjustments, downstream patch carry). True gate: `rewrite` — scope/approach of the implementation changes with the outcome. E002 carries `gated_by=E001`. |\n55\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates affected Implementations) | The same investigation determines whether the odh-model-controller authentication flow needs additional RBAC/TLS adjustments on OpenShift. True gate: `add_remediation` — the reconciler is needed in every outcome, but the investigation may reveal additional work. E003 carries `gated_by=E001`. |\n56\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (APIs/CRDs are build-against artifacts) | odh-model-controller declares `github.com/kserve/kserve` as a Go module dependency and already runs an LLMInferenceService controller and v1alpha2 mutating webhook (architecture context: odh-model-controller.md). E003's reconciler reacts to `spec.scaling.keda` — the kserve v1alpha2 API surface E002's work stream introduces — so its trigger-detection path can only compile once the standalone KEDA API types are available in the kserve version consumed downstream (via merged upstream #5839 or the downstream patch carry selected by E001). This is a build-against artifact requiring a serial edge, not a configuration reference. The reverse direction is different: the TriggerAuthentication resource E003 provisions is a runtime configuration reference consumed by user trigger definitions — E002's code does not build against it — so that relationship is captured as an acceptance criterion on E002, not an edge. |\n57\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | Documentation describes what was built; must reflect the delivered API surface and both autoscaling paths. |\n58\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | Docs must instruct operators to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift. |\n59\t| E004 not gated by E001 | Rule 11 scope | Rule 11 blocks docs on Implementation epics only; E001 is an Investigation and is already a transitive prerequisite via E002/E003. |\n60\t\n61\tUpstream external dependency handling (Rule 21): kserve/kserve#5839 is an in-progress\n62\tthird-party upstream PR. RHOAI is not the submitting party, and a viable fallback exists\n63\t(carry the patch downstream with backport once merged, per the strategy's risk mitigation).\n64\tIt is therefore modeled as a precondition captured as an acceptance criterion on E002, plus\n65\tthe E001 investigation to resolve adaptation needs — not as a separate upstream Implementation\n66\tepic. Tiered delivery applies (see below).\n67\t\n68\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n69\t\n70\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n71\t|-----|----------|---------|\n72\t| HLR-1: Standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without WVA | P0 | E002 (API/controller path), E003 (OpenShift Prometheus trigger authentication) |\n73\t| HLR-2: Mutual exclusivity of WVA and standalone KEDA via CEL validation | P0 | E002 |\n74\t| HLR-3: Scale-to-zero via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` (both paths) | P0 | E002 |\n75\t| HLR-4: Scale-from-zero (standalone KEDA via user-configured triggers) | P0 | E002 (mechanism), E004 (recommended trigger configurations) |\n76\t| HLR-5: Disaggregated prefill/decode independent scaling | P0 | E002 |\n77\t| HLR-6: Scaling events visible with signal attribution | P1 | E002 (status conditions/events for standalone path) |\n78\t| HLR-7: Decision-guide documentation covering both paths | P1 | E004 |\n79\t\n80\tAll 7 HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. Priority-split check for E002: HLR-6 (P1) is\n81\tbundled with the P0 work because scaling status conditions and events share the same\n82\tScalingSpec API surface and controller reconciliation path as the P0 work — it is not\n83\tindependently deferrable and does not warrant a separate epic.\n84\t\n85\t## Component Mapping\n86\t\n87\tCanonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`:\n88\t- \"KServe (llmisvc-controller)\" → **Serving Orchestration** (no \"KServe\" component exists; Serving Orchestration is the closest parent-level component for the serving controllers).\n89\t- \"odh-model-controller\" → **Serving Orchestration** (same mapping rationale; RHOAI serving-stack controller).\n90\t- \"Documentation\" → **Documentation** (exact match).\n91\t\n92\tBoth KServe (kserve.md) and odh-model-controller (odh-model-controller.md) exist in\n93\t`.context/architecture-context/` — both are in-platform components, so no onboarding chain\n94\t(Rule 20) is generated. E002 and E003 remain separate epics despite sharing component and\n95\tteam because they live in different repositories with different logical changes (Rule 1/14).\n96\t\n97\t## Unknown Handling Log\n98\t\n99\t| Unknown (source) | Disposition | Rationale |\n100\t|------------------|-------------|-----------|\n101\t| Upstream PR #5839 downstream adaptation needs (Open Questions) | Investigation E001, gating E002 (`rewrite`) and E003 (`add_remediation`) | Answer changes what the downstream implementations must include. |\n102\t| Target p50/p95 for scale-from-zero time (Open Questions, llm-d team) | Acceptance criterion on E004 | Determines documented headroom recommendations only; epic structure unchanged. |\n103\t| Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet scale (Open Questions, PSAP) | Acceptance criterion on E004 | Documentation content input; no structural impact. |\n104\t| KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` pause/resume behavior for LLM workloads (Assumption, needs validation) | Acceptance criterion (validation) on E002 | Scale-to-zero is a P0 HLR regardless of outcome; failure = fix-and-retry, epic structure unchanged. |\n105\t| EPP flow control metrics availability at zero replicas (Assumption, needs validation) | Acceptance criterion (validation) on E002 and content input to E004 | Scale-from-zero trigger mechanism is user-configured either way; affects documented guidance, not epic existence. |\n106\t| Upstream PR delay (Risk, mitigation: downstream patch carry) | Acceptance criterion on E002 | Viable fallback exists (Rule 21) — noted as AC, not a separate epic. |\n107\t\n108\t## Health Warnings\n109\t\n110\t- **Scalability NFR has no measurable target**: the strategy explicitly flags that no RHOAI-specific target exists for concurrent ScaledObjects. No numeric scalability AC can be derived; E004 carries capacity guidance instead. Human verification recommended.\n111\t- **AC 6 feasibility (metric outage → hold replica count)**: holding the current replica count during a Prometheus outage is KEDA operator behavior, not something the llmisvc-controller controls. Kept as an AC on E002 but flagged: if KEDA does not natively hold replicas on metric errors, this AC may be unsatisfiable within this strategy's scope.\n112\t- **Pending process gates**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, License Validation, etc.) are marked \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. These are strategy sign-off gates, not epic-level work; they inform E001's human-judgment signal but do not create epics.\n113\t- **Priority inversion check**: none found. P0 HLRs align with the core API/controller work; the P1 observability HLR is bundled for surface-coupling reasons (documented above), not deprioritized.\n114\t- **E002 sizing sits at the upper bound of the epic guideline**: the strategy estimates the\n115\t  llmisvc-controller chunk (API types, CEL validation, controller branching, status\n116\t  conditions, scale-to-zero/disaggregated support, cross-topology integration testing) at\n117\t  1-2 sprints. The epic is kept whole rather than split: the API types, validation, and\n118\t  controller branching share one API surface and one reconciliation path (splitting would\n119\t  create an artificial build-against chain within a single repo's change), and upstream PR\n120\t  kserve/kserve#5839 serves as the reference implementation, reducing implementation risk\n121\t  at the upper bound. Monitor during refinement: if the estimate firms up at 2+ sprints,\n122\t  split integration testing (cross-topology validation) into its own epic.\n123\t\n124\t## Tiered Delivery\n125\t\n126\tExternal dependency with uncertain timing: upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 (in progress).\n127\tPartial value can be delivered without it, so the work splits:\n128\t\n129\t- **Tier 1 (independent of upstream merge timing)**:\n130\t  - E001 — resolves adaptation needs and the patch-carry decision (risk-reduction).\n131\t  - E003 (auth-resource provisioning only) — the odh-model-controller\n132\t    TriggerAuthentication reconciler follows the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler`\n133\t    pattern, and its auth-resource provisioning logic (ServiceAccount, token Secret,\n134\t    Role/RoleBinding, TriggerAuthentication CR) is independent of when #5839 lands\n135\t    (risk-reduction work with independent value for the auth flow). Caveat: E003's\n136\t    trigger-detection path reads `spec.scaling.keda` and therefore compiles against\n137\t    the kserve v1alpha2 API types (a Go module dependency of odh-model-controller),\n138\t    so E003 as a whole cannot build and unit-test against typed fields until the\n139\t    standalone KEDA API types are available downstream — the E002 → E003 edge\n140\t    (Rule 13). The provisioning logic can be developed against the existing pattern\n141\t    in parallel and wired to the new API surface once the types land.\n142\t- **Tier 2 (gated on the upstream outcome)**:\n143\t  - E002 — the llmisvc-controller standalone KEDA path; consumes upstream #5839 (merged) or\n144\t    the downstream patch carry selected by E001.\n145\t  - E003 (trigger-detection wiring) — the typed `spec.scaling.keda` trigger-detection\n146\t    path, blocked by E002's delivery of the API types (Rule 13 build-against).\n147\t  - E004 — documentation of what was built (blocked by all implementations per Rule 11).\n148\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-932df1256081f5ad","agent_id":"a23ebb4bbaff35dfc","session_id":"c67b7b09-8e50-4c38-b7b7-230c45313c19","uuid":"88cec10d-75e3-43c0-a314-5d46839e15ea","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:43:51.173Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a24187e822425a0f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
