{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7afce8574415487","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8008-E003\n3\ttitle: KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA\n4\t  in odh-model-controller\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n6\tcomponent: Serving Orchestration\n7\tteam: Serving team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8008-E002\n13\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: add_remediation\n16\t  fallback_approach: The reconciler is needed in every outcome of the upstream adaptation\n17\t    investigation; if the investigation reveals additional RBAC or TLS adjustments\n18\t    required for OpenShift beyond the authentication context, add them to this epic's\n19\t    scope rather than restructuring it.\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: -1\n26\t  external_dependency: 0\n27\t  human_process_gates: 0\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 5\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\tbranch: null\n36\t---\n37\t# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA in odh-model-controller\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tProvides the downstream authentication context that KEDA ScaledObjects need to query\n42\tThanos/Prometheus on OpenShift for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA deployments with\n43\tPrometheus-type triggers. On OpenShift, KEDA cannot query Prometheus without an\n44\tauthenticated context; this epic adds a parallel reconciler flow in odh-model-controller\n45\tthat provisions the per-namespace authentication resources, following the existing\n46\t`kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern used for v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n47\t\n48\t## Scope\n49\t\n50\t- A reconciler flow in odh-model-controller (extending the pattern at\n51\t  `internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go`) that reacts to\n52\t  LLMInferenceService resources with the standalone KEDA path configured and\n53\t  Prometheus-type triggers, and provisions per-namespace authentication resources:\n54\t  - a dedicated ServiceAccount (`inference-prometheus-auth`),\n55\t  - a ServiceAccountToken Secret (`kubernetes.io/service-account-token` type),\n56\t  - a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access,\n57\t  - a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying the bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's\n58\t    Prometheus triggers.\n59\t- Resource naming, owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup consistent with the\n60\t  existing v1beta1 reconciler (resources are cleaned up when the owning\n61\t  LLMInferenceService is deleted or its KEDA configuration removed).\n62\t- Any RBAC or TLS adjustments for OpenShift identified by the upstream adaptation\n63\t  investigation, folded into this reconciler flow as additional scope.\n64\t- TLS 1.2+ for Prometheus/Thanos queries via the TriggerAuthentication CR (no new\n65\t  authentication surfaces introduced).\n66\t- The reconciler's trigger-detection path (reacting to `spec.scaling.keda` with\n67\t  Prometheus-type triggers) compiles against the kserve v1alpha2 API types, which\n68\t  odh-model-controller consumes as a Go module dependency\n69\t  (`github.com/kserve/kserve`, per architecture context). The standalone KEDA API\n70\t  types must be available in the kserve version consumed downstream — via the\n71\t  merged upstream kserve/kserve#5839 or the downstream patch carry — before this\n72\t  code can build; this is a build-against dependency on the standalone KEDA\n73\t  controller work stream, not a configuration reference.\n74\t\n75\tOut of scope: the llmisvc-controller ScaledObject creation itself, KEDA installation\n76\t(platform policy overlay 0008 — documented as a prerequisite), and documentation of the\n77\tTriggerAuthentication reference in user trigger definitions (documentation work stream).\n78\t\n79\t## Acceptance Criteria\n80\t\n81\t- Given an LLMInferenceService with `spec.scaling.keda` including a Prometheus-type\n82\t  trigger, when the odh-model-controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication\n83\t  resources are created (ServiceAccount, token Secret, Role/RoleBinding,\n84\t  TriggerAuthentication CR), measured by `kubectl get` on each resource type in the\n85\t  target namespace.\n86\t- Given a created TriggerAuthentication CR, when KEDA evaluates the Prometheus trigger,\n87\t  then the query succeeds with the ServiceAccount bearer token over TLS 1.2+, measured by\n88\t  KEDA ScaledObject reporting the trigger as active and returning metric values.\n89\t- Given an LLMInferenceService is deleted or its standalone KEDA configuration removed,\n90\t  when the controller reconciles, then the owned authentication resources are cleaned up,\n91\t  measured by no orphaned ServiceAccounts, Secrets, RoleBindings, or\n92\t  TriggerAuthentication CRs remaining.\n93\t- Deployments without Prometheus-type triggers, without standalone KEDA configured, or\n94\t  with WVA configured are unaffected, measured by no authentication resources created for\n95\t  them.\n96\t- Any RBAC/TLS adjustments for OpenShift surfaced by the upstream adaptation\n97\t  investigation are implemented (or explicitly recorded as not required in the findings).\n98\t- Given the reconciler's trigger-detection path compiles against the kserve v1alpha2 API\n99\t  types, when the code is built, then the kserve version consumed by\n100\t  odh-model-controller (Go module `github.com/kserve/kserve`) includes the standalone\n101\t  KEDA `spec.scaling.keda` API surface — delivered either by the merged upstream\n102\t  kserve/kserve#5839 or by the downstream patch carry — measured by a successful\n103\t  compilation and unit tests exercising the typed `spec.scaling.keda` fields\n104\t  (precondition: the standalone KEDA controller work stream has made the API types\n105\t  available downstream).\n106\t\n107\t## HLR Traceability\n108\t\n109\t- HLR-1 (P0): standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without WVA — this\n110\t  epic enables the primary supported trigger source (Prometheus) for the standalone path\n111\t  on OpenShift.\n112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-932df1256081f5ad","agent_id":"a23ebb4bbaff35dfc","session_id":"c67b7b09-8e50-4c38-b7b7-230c45313c19","uuid":"93024f88-6631-4785-a117-660ca643516d","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:44:01.335Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7afce8574415487","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
